  "THE SYMBOLS": "",
  "Cho ku rei": "",
  "<p>It directs energy to the physical body. It goes by several names, such as “the power is here”. It is a symbol of power and acts as a switch that opens the flow of energy: “put all the power of the universe here”.</p><p>Curiously, it can both amplify energy and reduce it. Drawn clockwise it amplifies (for loss of balance, for example); drawn anticlockwise it reduces, as with Parkinson's or nervous tics, or a growth or disorder one wishes to lessen.</p><p>It is drawn with the middle finger or the palm of the hand, in a single stroke. It can also be visualised in the mind.</p><p>Using it is simple: draw it once and recite the mantra three times, very softly or in your head. It is used to shorten a treatment, cleanse a place, or focus work on an organ, by drawing it several times and reciting the mantra over the affected spot.</p>": "",
  "The Cho ku rei symbol, with its stroke order": "",
  "<p><u>Common USES of the symbol</u></p><p>Because it works on the physical level, it is used not only for illnesses patients may have but also for injuries they may have suffered.</p><ul><li>Easing pain from injuries.</li><li>Improving concentration, “grounding” yourself.</li><li>Strengthening one's healing abilities.</li></ul>": "",
  "<p><u>Its use is not limited to the individual’s physical body but extends to the space around the person</u>, and so:</p><ul><li>Drawing a space around the person receiving treatment to keep negative energies away.</li><li>Clearing a room of negative energy by drawing or visualising the symbol on doors, corners, ceiling and floor; energy moves in circles and gathers in corners.</li></ul>": "",
  "The Cho ku rei laid in pebbles in the labyrinth": "",
  "<ul><li>Over food and water, which, if drained of energy, become wholesome and healing.</li><li>Over medicines, to boost their good effects and lessen possible side effects.</li><li>To cleanse a place, draw it at the entrance facing inwards; once inside, draw it on each wall, the ceiling and the floor to protect and seal.</li></ul>": "",
  "<ul><li>For protecting yourself, your family, your home, your car, doors and windows.</li><li>For ailments that come from overwhelming anger, hatred or sadness.</li><li>Whenever a negative thought comes, draw it at once.</li><li>It is recommended in every treatment, especially in the first days, for a deep cleanse. In short, it works mainly on the physical body.</li><li>For bringing abundance: picture clearly what you are asking for, without forcing anyone's free will.</li></ul>": "",
  "<p class=\"fb-verse\">I ask for this, or its equivalent, or something better, in keeping with free will, so long as it harms no one and all is for the good.</p><p class=\"fb-note\">This text records the Reiki tradition as it appears in the book; it is not medical advice.</p>": "",
  "Yin and Yang": "",
  "<p>It comes from Chinese philosophy, though it runs through much of Eastern thought. It is a cosmological principle that explains the duality of everything in the universe. Everything has its opposite, but not absolutely, only relatively, since nothing is entirely yin or entirely yang.</p><p>Balance comes from placing a part of each inside the other, with a winding line between them that stands for the alternation of forces. A circle surrounds and closes the whole symbol, standing for the All. That is how complete harmony is reached.</p>": "",
  "Yin and yang laid in white and black pebbles": "",
  "<ul><li>There is yang in yin and yin in yang: each is always within the other.</li><li>They form a dynamic balance: as one grows, the other shrinks. Water turns to vapour and rises, but too much vapour in the clouds ends up as rain.</li><li>They depend on each other: neither can exist alone. There is no day without night.</li><li>What is hot (yang) can be boiling or lukewarm; what is cold (yin) can be cool or frozen.</li><li>They turn into their opposites: night becomes day, warmth cools, a birth brings a death.</li><li>They oppose each other, but relatively: winter opposes summer, though a summer day can be cold.</li></ul>": "",
  "The solar cross in mosaic": "",
  "<p>It may well be the oldest religious symbol in the world, found in Asian, American, Indian and European religions, pagan ones included. A cross within a circle is the simplest possible picture of opposite poles joined together.</p><p>It stands for the sun, and each of its four parts is a season, the two equinoxes and two solstices that make up the Wheel of the Year; also the four elements (air, fire, water, earth), the four directions, the four winds…</p><p>It also speaks of opposites set against each other: earth and sky, life and death, up and down, right and left, active and passive, feminine (horizontal) and masculine (vertical).</p>": "",
  "The triskele .-": "",
  "<p>Its name comes from Breton Celtic and means “three wings”: three spirals turning around a centre. Three is the most sacred number in Celtic culture, standing for perfection and balance.</p><p>It is a solar symbol for the three elements (air, earth and water), the three dimensions (height, length and width), the three parts of a being (body, spirit and soul), or the three ages of life (child, youth and old age).</p>": "",
  "The triskele in mosaic": "",
  "<p>It was the druids' highest symbol, and only they could wear it; to them it meant learning, past, present and future. It also stands for the balance of body, mind and spirit, beginning and end, evolution and growth. It was used to heal wounds and ease fevers, and worn as a talisman of fertility, renewal and life force.</p><p>In Asturias, since medieval times, triskeles were carved or painted on the wooden walls of hórreos, the raised granaries, to protect the harvest stored inside.</p>": "",
  "FOOT REFLEXOLOGY": "",
  "<p>Two foot-reflexology circles, a star and a flower, invite you to take your shoes off and treat your feet to a relaxing massage.</p><p>A sign helps you notice which parts of the body are lowest in energy, which your feet give away through their most sensitive spots.</p>": "",
